NNAAMMEE
       rename - rename files

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       rreennaammee [options] _e_x_p_r_e_s_s_i_o_n _r_e_p_l_a_c_e_m_e_n_t _f_i_l_e...

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       rreennaammee will rename the specified files by replacing the first
       occurrence of _e_x_p_r_e_s_s_i_o_n in their name by _r_e_p_l_a_c_e_m_e_n_t.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --ss, ----ssyymmlliinnkk
           Do not rename a symlink but its target.

       --vv, ----vveerrbboossee
           Show which files were renamed, if any.

       --nn, ----nnoo--aacctt
           Do not make any changes; add ----vveerrbboossee to see what would be made.

       --aa, ----aallll
           Replace all occurrences of _e_x_p_r_e_s_s_i_o_n rather than only the first
           one.

       --ll, ----llaasstt
           Replace the last occurrence of _e_x_p_r_e_s_s_i_o_n rather than the first
           one.

       --oo, ----nnoo--oovveerrwwrriittee
           Do not overwrite existing files. When ----ssyymmlliinnkk is active, do not
           overwrite symlinks pointing to existing targets.

       --ii, ----iinntteerraaccttiivvee
           Ask before overwriting existing files.

       --hh, ----hheellpp
           Display help text and exit.

       --VV, ----vveerrssiioonn
           Print version and exit.

WWAARRNNIINNGG
       The renaming has no safeguards by default or without any one of the
       options ----nnoo--oovveerrwwrriittee, ----iinntteerraaccttiivvee or ----nnoo--aacctt. If the user has
       permission to rewrite file names, the command will perform the action
       without any questions. For example, the result can be quite drastic
       when the command is run as root in the _/_l_i_b directory. Always make a
       backup before running the command, unless you truly know what you are
       doing.

IINNTTEERRAACCTTIIVVEE MMOODDEE
       As most standard utilities rename can be used with a terminal device
       (tty in short) in canonical mode, where the line is buffered by the tty
       and you press ENTER to validate the user input. If you put your tty in
       cbreak mode however, rename requires only a single key press to answer
       the prompt. To set cbreak mode, run for example:

           sh -c 'stty -icanon min 1; "$0" "$@"; stty icanon' rename -i from to files

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
       00
           all requested rename operations were successful

       11
           all rename operations failed

       22
           some rename operations failed

       44
           nothing was renamed

       6644
           unanticipated error occurred

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
       Given the files _f_o_o_1, ..., _f_o_o_9, _f_o_o_1_0, ..., _f_o_o_2_7_8, the commands

           rename foo foo00 foo?
           rename foo foo0 foo??

       will turn them into _f_o_o_0_0_1, ..., _f_o_o_0_0_9, _f_o_o_0_1_0, ..., _f_o_o_2_7_8. And

           rename .htm .html *.htm

       will fix the extension of your html files. Provide an empty string for
       shortening:

           rename '_with_long_name' '' file_with_long_name.*

       will remove the substring in the filenames.

